+# Coupled status
+
+Inspect a native coupled run without contacting a provider or changing the run:
+
+```sh
+spiral-coder coupled-status --run-dir PATH
+```
+
+The installed npm entry forwards the same command and arguments:
+
+```sh
+npx spiral-coder coupled-status --run-dir PATH
+```
+
+`--run-dir` is resolved relative to the caller's current directory. The command
+reads `iterations.json` and durable `state/tui-work.json` ledgers only. A wire
+request is every durable request record; a task request is a record charged to
+the task. When native records omit `charge`, that request uses the native task
+charge default. Thus task and wire counts can differ, and failed or in-flight
+requests still count.
+
+An interrupted iteration may have an absent ledger. In that case its usage is
+unknown and the report uses `null` for the pending counts and aggregate used and
+remaining totals; unknown usage is never treated as zero. Limits remain known.
+
+The `completed` status is the recorded native completion state. It is not an
+independent verification, qualification, or promotion authorization decision.
+
+The following is the completed synthetic example produced by
+`scripts.status_fixture.make` and `scripts.status_fixture.expected`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "version": 1,
+  "status": "completed",
+  "stop_reason": "completed",
+  "iterations": [
+    {
+      "iteration": 1,
+      "task_id": "t1",
+      "termination": "completed",
+      "task_requests": 2,
+      "wire_requests": 3,
+      "changed_files": ["src/a.rs"],
+      "newly_verified": ["cargo test -q"]
+    }
+  ],
+  "pending": null,
+  "requests": {
+    "task_used": 2,
+    "wire_used": 3,
+    "task_limit": 192,
+    "wire_limit": 216,
+    "task_remaining": 190,
+    "wire_remaining": 213
+  }
+}
+```
